"Web Animations Module Level 1 (https://drafts.csswg.org/web-animations-1/)\nciteturn3619view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/web-animations-1/\",\"pattern\":\"If the iteration progress is unresolved\"}); Total lines: 4503\nL1510: 【333†Image: Effect of iterations and fill on iteration time.】 After one iteration, the 【183†iteration progress】 is 0, but after two iterations (and there onwards), the iteration progress is 1 due to the special behavior defined when an animation effect fills.\nL1610: If the animation effect is in the 【286†before phase】, let `overall progress` be zero; otherwise, let it be equal to the 【282†iteration count】.\nL1611: \nL1612: Otherwise,\nL1613: \nL1614: \nL1615: Let `overall progress` be the result of calculating `【291†active time】 / 【176†iteration duration】`.\nL1616: \nL1617:   3. Return the result of calculating ``overall progress` + 【283†iteration start】`.\nL1618: \nL1619: Tests\nL1620: \nL1621:   * 【346†overallProgress.html†wpt.fyi】 【347†(live test)†wpt.live】 【348†(source)†github.com】\nL1622: #### 4.7.4. Calculating the simple iteration progress\nL1623: \nL1624: The simple iteration progress is a fraction of the progress through the current iteration that ignores transformations to the time introduced by the 【284†playback direction】 or 【336†easing functions】 applied to the effect, and is calculated as follows:\nL1625: \nL1626:   1. If the 【344†overall progress】 is 【180†unresolved】, return unresolved.\nL1627:   2. If 【344†overall progress】 is infinity, let the `simple iteration progress` be `【283†iteration start】 % 1.0`; otherwise, let the `simple iteration progress` be `【344†overall progress】 % 1.0`.\nL1628: \nL1629:   3. If all of the following conditions are true:\nL1630: \nL1631:      * the `simple iteration progress` calculated above is zero, and\nL1632: \nL1633:      * the animation effect is in the 【301†active phase】 or the 【287†after phase】, and\nL1634: \nL1635:      * the 【291†active time】 is equal to the 【281†active duration】, and\nL1636:      * the 【282†iteration count】 is not equal to zero.\nL1637: \nL1638: Let the `simple iteration progress` be 1.0.\nL1639: \nL1640: The above step implements the behavior that when an animation’s active interval ends precisely at the end of an iteration, it fills by holding the endpoint of the final iteration rather than the start of the next iteration.\nL1641: \nL1642: The final condition prevents this from applying when we never played any iterations of the animation to begin with because the 【282†iteration count】 was zero.\nL1643:   4. Return `simple iteration progress`.\nL1644: #### 4.7.5. Calculating the 【184†current iteration index】\nL1645: \nL1646: The current iteration index can be calculated using the following steps:\nL1647: \nL1648:   1. If the 【291†active time】 is 【180†unresolved】, return unresolved.\nL1649: \nL1650:   2. If the animation effect is in the 【287†after phase】 and the 【282†iteration count】 is infinity, return infinity.\nL1651: \nL1652:   3. If the 【345†simple iteration progress】 is 1.0, return `floor(【344†overall progress】) − 1`.\nL1653: \nL1654:   4. Otherwise, return `floor(【344†overall progress】)`.\nL1655: #### 4.7.6. Calculating the directed progress\nL1656: \nL1657: The directed progress is calculated from the 【345†simple iteration progress】 using the following steps:\nL1658: \nL1659:   1. If the 【345†simple iteration progress】 is 【180†unresolved】, return unresolved.\nL1660: \nL1661:   2. Calculate the `current direction` using the first matching condition from the following list:\nL1662: \nL1663: If 【284†playback direction】 is 【349†normal】,\nL1664: \nL1665: \nL1666: Let the `current direction` be forwards.\nL1667: \nL1668: If 【284†playback direction】 is 【350†reverse】,\nL1669: Let the `current direction` be reverse.\nL1670: \nL1671: Otherwise (the 【284†playback direction】 is 【351†alternate】 or 【352†alternate-reverse】)p,\nL1672: \nL1673:     1. Let `d` be the 【184†current iteration index】.\nL1674: \nL1675:     2. If 【284†playback direction】 is 【352†alternate-reverse】 increment `d` by 1.\nL1676: \nL1677:     3. If ``d` % 2 == 0`, let the `current direction` be forwards; otherwise let the `current direction` be reverse. If `d` is infinity, let the `current direction` be forwards.\nL1678:   3. If the `current direction` is forwards then return the 【345†simple iteration progress】.\nL1679: \nL1680: Otherwise, return `1.0 − 【345†simple iteration progress】`.\nL1681: #### 4.7.7. Calculating the transformed progress\nL1682: \nL1683: The transformed progress is calculated from the 【334†directed progress】 using the following steps:\nL1684: \nL1685:   1. If the 【334†directed progress】 is 【180†unresolved】, return unresolved.\nL1686: \nL1687:   2. Calculate the value of the `before flag` as follows:\nL1688: \nL1689:     1. Determine the `current direction` using the procedure defined in 【92†§ 4.7.6 Calculating the directed progress】.\nL1690: \nL1691:     2. If the `current direction` is forwards, let `going forwards` be true; otherwise it is false.\nL1692:     3. The `before flag` is set if the animation effect is in the 【286†before phase】 and `going forwards` is true; or if the animation effect is in the 【287†after phase】 and `going forwards` is false.\nL1693: \nL1694:   3. Return the result of evaluating the 【338†effect easing function】 passing 【334†directed progress】 as the 【353†input progress value】 and `before flag` as the 【354†before flag】.\nL1695: #### 4.7.8. Yielding the iteration progress\nL1696: \nL1697: The final output iteration progress of an 【174†animation effect】 is simply its 【342†transformed progress】.\nL1698: ## 5. Animation model\nL1699: \nL1700: This section is non-normative\nL1701: \nL1702: For some kinds of 【174†animation effects】, the Web Animations 【182†animation model】 takes the 【183†iteration progress】 and 【184†iteration index】 values produced by the 【185†timing model】 and uses them to calculate a corresponding output.\nL1703: \nL1704: The output of each such animation effect is then combined with that of others using an 【355†effect stack】 before being applied to the 【186†target properties】 (see 【104†§ 5.4 Combining effects】).\nL1705: ### 5.1. Introduction\nL1706: \nL1707: An 【174†animation effect】 has zero or more associated properties that it affects in response to changes to its timing output. These properties are referred to as the effect’s target properties.\nL1708: \nL1709: Given an 【183†iteration progress】, an 【184†iteration index】, and an 【356†underlying value】, an 【174†animation effect】 produces an effect value for each 【357†animatable】 【186†target property】 by applying the procedures from the 【358†animation type】 appropriate to the property.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWeb Animations Module Level 1 (https://drafts.csswg.org/web-animations-1/)\nciteturn3619view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/web-animations-1/\",\"pattern\":\"5.4. Combining effects\"}); Total lines: 4503\nL131:       4. 【103†5.3.4 The effect value of a keyframe effect】\nL132:     4. 【104†5.4 Combining effects】\nL133:       1. 【105†5.4.1 Animation classes】\nL134:       2. 【106†5.4.2 The effect stack】\nL135:       3. 【107†5.4.3 Calculating the result of an effect stack】\nL136:       4. 【108†5.4.4 Effect composition】\nL137:       5. 【109†5.4.5 Applying the composited result】\nL138:     5. 【110†5.5 Replacing animations】\nL139:       1. 【111†5.5.1 Replace state】\nL140:       2. 【112†5.5.2 Removing replaced animations】\nL141:     6. 【113†5.6 Side effects of animation】\nL151:       2. 【123†6.5.2 The `FillMode` enumeration】\nL152:       3. 【124†6.5.3 The `PlaybackDirection` enumeration】\nL153:       4. 【125†6.5.4 Updating the timing of an `AnimationEffect`】\nL154:       5. 【126†6.5.5 The `ComputedEffectTiming` dictionary】\nL155:     6. 【127†6.6 The `KeyframeEffect` interface】\nL156:       1. 【128†6.6.1 Creating a new `KeyframeEffect` object】\nL157:       2. 【129†6.6.2 Property names and IDL names】\nL158:       3. 【130†6.6.3 Processing a `keyframes` argument】\nL159:       4. 【131†6.6.4 The `KeyframeEffectOptions` dictionary】\nL1698: ## 5. Animation model\nL1699: \nL1700: This section is non-normative\nL1701: \nL1702: For some kinds of 【174†animation effects】, the Web Animations 【182†animation model】 takes the 【183†iteration progress】 and 【184†iteration index】 values produced by the 【185†timing model】 and uses them to calculate a corresponding output.\nL1703: \nL1704: The output of each such animation effect is then combined with that of others using an 【355†effect stack】 before being applied to the 【186†target properties】 (see 【104†§ 5.4 Combining effects】).\nL1906: ### 5.4. Combining effects\nL1907: \nL1908: This section is non-normative\nL1909: \nL1910: After calculating the 【378†effect values】 for a 【237†keyframe effect】, they are applied to the 【174†animation effect】’s 【186†target properties】.\nL1923: #### 5.4.1. Animation classes\nL1924: \nL1925: This specification provides a common animation model intended to be used by other specifications that define markup or programming interfaces on top of this model. The particular markup or programming interface that generated an 【173†animation】 defines its animation class.\nL1926: \nL1927: Further specifications may define specialized behavior for composite ordering between different classes of animations or within a particular class.\nL1928: \nL1929: This section is non-normative\nL1933: #### 5.4.2. The effect stack\nL1934: \nL1935: An effect stack is associated with each property 【186†targeted】 by one or more 【237†keyframe effects】. The 【355†effect stack】 establishes the relative composite order of keyframe effects.\nL1936: \nL1937: The relative composite order of any two 【237†keyframe effects】 `A` and `B` within an 【355†effect stack】 is established by comparing their properties as follows:\nL1938: \nL1939:   1. Sort `A` and `B` by applying the following conditions in turn until the order is resolved,\nL1946: #### 5.4.3. Calculating the result of an effect stack\nL1947: \nL1948: In order to calculate the final value of an 【355†effect stack】, the 【378†effect values】 of each 【237†keyframe effect】 in the stack are combined in composite order.\nL1949: \nL1950: Each step in the process of evaluating an 【355†effect stack】 takes an underlying value as input.\nL1953: #### 5.4.4. Effect composition\nL1954: \nL1955: The specific operation used to combine an 【378†effect value】 with an【356†underlying value】 is determined by the composite operation of the 【237†keyframe effect】 that produced the effect value.\nL1956: \nL1957: This specification defines three 【381†composite operations】 as follows:\nL1958: \nL1959: replace\nL1960: \nL1961: \nL1962: The result of compositing the 【378†effect value】 with the 【356†underlying value】 is simply the effect value.\nL1963: \nL1964: add\nL1973: #### 5.4.5. Applying the composited result\nL1974: \nL1975: Applying a 【453†composited value】 to a 【186†target property】 is achieved by adding a specified value to the CSS cascade.\nL1976: \nL1977: The level of the cascade to which this specified value is added depends on the 【226†class】 of the 【173†animation】 【288†associated with】 the effect with the highest composite order in the 【355†effect stack】 for a given property. By default, the specified value is added to the 【454†Animation Origin】 of the cascade. 【324†[CSS-CASCADE-3]】\nL1978: \nL1979: Tests\nL4037:   * 【206†timeline time to origin-relative time】, in § 4.3\nL4038:   * 【290†time spaces】, in § 4.6.4\nL4039:   * 【189†time value】, in § 4.2\nL4040:   * 【181†timing model】, in § 3\nL4041:   * 【192†timing nodes】, in § 4\nL4042:   * 【342†transformed progress】, in § 4.7.7\nL4043:   * 【356†underlying value】, in § 5.4.3\nL4044:   * 【180†unresolved】, in § 4.2\nL4045:   * 【213†update an animation’s finished state】, in § 